The cell voltage is a combination of the anodic voltage and the cathodic voltage. In particular, the cathodic voltage with respect to the anodic voltage. For the cell formed with V and Au, we have:\\
 
\begin{raggedleft} \ce{	3V_{(s)} + 2Au^{3+}_{(aq)}  + 6e^- <=>	 3V^{2+}_{(aq)} + 2Au_{(s)}+ 6e^-} \end{raggedleft} 
The following equation is used to calculate the cell voltage from the separate electronic potentials:
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\mathcal{E}^{\circ}_{cell}=\mathcal{E}^{\circ}_{cathode}-\mathcal{E}^{\circ}_{anode}
   }\label{\chapterlabel:equation1}
\end{equation}  
Hence, the cell volatage for the V and Au cell would be:
\begin{center}$\mathcal{E}^{\circ}_{cathode}-\mathcal{E}^{\circ}_{anode}=$1.13+1.52=2.65V\end{center}